before:
This practice checks a simplified restored snapshot. Version must match, and every note ID/title pair must match.

```javascript
const rows = [{id:1}, {id:2}];
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id > 0));
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id === 1));
```
This prints true, then false. every receives a predicate called on successive entries and returns whether all tested entries pass, stopping on the first failure. An empty array returns true; it has no failing entry. It does not validate a backup version by itself.

after:
every checks whether all entries pass a rule.

```javascript
const rows = [{id:1}, {id:2}];
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id > 0));
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id === 1));
```

The first rule accepts both rows, so it returns true. The second fails on ID 2, so it returns false. An empty array returns true because no entry fails.
